 "case:briefing.0": "The Glenmorran Hotel, at the head of Glen Morran in the Highlands. Sunday 25 January 2026: Burns Night. You came up on Friday for the hotel’s famous Burns supper and a few days’ walking. Queen Victoria took tea here, The Grey Lady of Glenmorran was filmed in its Great Hall, and its bar keeps four hundred whiskies. Since half past nine last night, black ice has closed the pass, and the hotel is cut off.",
 "case:briefing.1": "At the supper the film’s star, Dame Vivienne Lisle, 78, did something nobody expected. She took her famous black diamond, the Black Ice, out of its display case and fastened it round the neck of the head housekeeper, Halina Kowalczyk, ‘Mrs K’ to three generations of guests. ‘For twenty winters this woman has looked after me,’ she said. ‘It’s hers.’ Mrs K went up to turn down the first-floor beds, wearing it.",
 "case:briefing.2": "At two minutes to midnight the lights went out: the storm, everyone said. You sat in the Great Hall with everyone else, in candlelight, while Dame Vivienne sang Burns. At 00:24 the night porter got the lights back. At 06:40 this morning, Mrs K’s daughter Kasia found her mother dead on the floor of the linen room. The Black Ice was gone.",
 "case:briefing.3": "The police will come up the glen behind the gritters this afternoon. Until then nobody can leave, and whoever killed Mrs K is still in the hotel. Kasia has asked you to find out who it was before the police arrive and the guests scatter. ‘My mother noticed everything,’ she says. ‘Nobody ever noticed her.’",
 "case:briefing.4": "Alasdair Munro, the owner, has given you the run of the hotel, and Dr Morven Shaw, a GP who was at the supper, has examined Mrs K. Everyone who was in the Glenmorran last night is still here.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "The gritters reached the pass at half past twelve, and the police came up the glen behind them at ten past two. Peggy Rennie was waiting for them in the library in her good coat, with her handbag on her knees. She told them everything, in order, as if she were reading out the running list for a quick change. That evening she was charged with Halina Kowalczyk’s murder.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "In the storm of 1998 she had held the torch while old Mr Dunbar, the handyman, put the big switch back, and she never forgot where anything was at the Glenmorran. On Saturday she switched the hotel off at two minutes to midnight, climbed the back stairs by the light of the pencil torch she had worn in the wings for forty years, and found Halina restocking pillowcases. ‘It’s mine, Hal,’ Peggy told her. ‘She promised it to me.’ ‘Then ask her,’ Halina said, and turned her back. Afterwards Peggy switched Halina’s torch off, because a light on that floor would have brought somebody running, and slipped back to her room as Tomasz came up the main stairs with his candle, calling for Mrs K. She dropped the stone in her tooth mug, filled it at the basin and set it out on the ledge in the snow. By morning it was what Archie had always called it: black ice.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "Dame Vivienne gave the Black Ice to Kasia. Kasia sold it in Antwerp in March. The money paid off the Glenmorran’s bank, and in return the people who work there, from Hamish to the kitchen porter, now own a third of it. Dame Vivienne died at the hotel in April, in the Victoria Suite, with Kasia reading to her.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "Rory goes to a meeting in Wandsworth every Tuesday, and hasn’t placed a bet since January. Alasdair Munro still runs the Burns supper. Tomasz still runs the bar, and on Sundays he cooks pierogi for Kasia, the way her mother used to cook for him. Hamish retired at Easter. Before he went, he screwed a brass plate to the plant-room door: KEEP LOCKED. ASK THE PORTER.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "At the next Burns supper, when Alasdair rose for the toast to the lassies, he raised his glass to Mrs K instead, and the whole hall stood. Upstairs, the first-floor beds had been turned down by her daughter: the corners folded back, a hot water bottle under every sheet, and a round of shortbread on every pillow.",
