 "case:title": "Black Ice",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_al_hall.insight": "At 23:45, on the first floor, Mrs K wrote on her list: ‘Mr M came up. The bank. NO.’",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_al_hall.response.0": "…She wrote it down. Of course she did.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_al_hall.response.1": "The bank takes the Glenmorran in March. A hundred and six years of Munros, and I’m the one who loses it. So I went up and asked her to lend us the stone as security, just until the summer. She said no, and she was right.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_al_hall.response.2": "I was back in the hall inside five minutes. I’m in the middle of the band’s photo at three minutes to midnight. Look at it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_al_hall.title": "Mr M came up",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_ham_storm.insight": "The staff cottage, on the same cable up the glen, never lost its power, so it wasn’t the storm. And by Hamish’s own card a tripped lever sits halfway, and has to go down before it goes up. He says he just pushed it up.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_ham_storm.response.0": "…Aye. I’ve been telling myself all morning it was the storm. It wasnae, was it?",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_ham_storm.response.1": "And I’ll tell ye what I didn’t stop to think about. When I got to the plant room, the lever wasn’t halfway. A trip leaves it halfway; I wrote the card on the wall myself. It was right down. Off. I was frozen and in a hurry, with a hotel full of folk in the dark, so I pushed it up and wrote ‘tripped’ in the book, like every other winter.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_ham_storm.response.2": "And the door was unlocked, with the key from the boot room still in it. I lock that door myself. Anybody who’s been here long enough knows where that key lives.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_ham_storm.title": "Not the storm",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.insight": "The Black Ice, torn from Mrs K’s neck, is frozen into Miss Rennie’s tooth mug on Miss Rennie’s window ledge. She says she went straight to her room and never went near the linen room.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.response.0": "…You looked out of the window. Nobody looks out of the window.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.response.1": "In the storm of 1998 I held the torch for old Mr Dunbar, the handyman, while he put the big switch back. I’ve never forgotten anything about this hotel. Last night I went down the back stairs, took the key from the boot room, and pushed the switch down.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.response.2": "I only meant to take it in the dark. She’d never have known who. But she had her torch, and she put it on my face and said, ‘Peggy?’ So I told her. ‘It’s mine, Hal. She promised it to me.’ And she said, ‘Then ask her,’ and turned back to her pillowcases, as if I’d come for an extra towel.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.response.3": "She’d put the torch down on the shelf. I hit her once. I took the chain, and I switched her torch off, because a light on that floor would have brought somebody. Then I heard Tomasz on the main stairs, calling for her, and I went to my room. I put the stone in my tooth mug, filled it at the basin, and set it out in the snow.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.response.4": "Forty-five years I’ve dressed that woman. She gave it away with a speech, and I clapped.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room.title": "Black ice",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.insight": "The Black Ice, torn from Mrs K’s neck, is frozen into Miss Rennie’s tooth mug on Miss Rennie’s window ledge. She says she never saw Mrs K.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.response.0": "…You looked out of the window. Nobody looks out of the window.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.response.1": "In the storm of 1998 I held the torch for old Mr Dunbar, the handyman, while he put the big switch back. I’ve never forgotten anything about this hotel. Last night I went down the back stairs, took the key from the boot room, and pushed the switch down.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.response.2": "I only meant to take it in the dark. She’d never have known who. But she had her torch, and she put it on my face and said, ‘Peggy?’ So I told her. ‘It’s mine, Hal. She promised it to me.’ And she said, ‘Then ask her,’ and turned back to her pillowcases, as if I’d come for an extra towel.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.response.3": "She’d put the torch down on the shelf. I hit her once. I took the chain, and I switched her torch off, because a light on that floor would have brought somebody. Then I heard Tomasz on the main stairs, calling for her, and I went to my room. I put the stone in my tooth mug, filled it at the basin, and set it out in the snow.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.response.4": "Forty-five years I’ve dressed that woman. She gave it away with a speech, and I clapped.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_room_k.title": "Black ice",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_suite.insight": "At 23:52 Mrs K turned down the Victoria Suite and Miss Rennie’s room, and wrote ‘Nobody in’. Miss Rennie says she was in the suite from half past eleven.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_suite.response.0": "…No. Not at that moment, I wasn’t. How silly of me.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_suite.response.1": "I’d gone down to the kitchens to heat some milk for Vivienne. She sleeps better with it. I was down there when the lights went out, and I felt my way back up the back stairs in the dark.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_suite.response.2": "I never saw Halina. If I’d known she was up there on her own…",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_suite.title": "Nobody in",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_torch.insight": "At about eight minutes past twelve Tomasz saw a thin torch beam, like a pencil, go into the Victoria Suite. Miss Rennie wears a pencil torch round her neck, and says she felt her way up in the dark.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_torch.response.0": "Her hand goes to the pencil torch on its cord.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_torch.response.1": "…Yes. I always have it with me. I said I felt my way because it sounded braver than the truth, which is that I was frightened, and I used my torch all the way up.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_torch.response.2": "I came up the back stairs and went straight to my room, and I sat on my bed until the lights came back. I didn’t stop anywhere, and I didn’t see Halina. I’d give anything now to have seen her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_peggy_torch.title": "A pencil of light",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_rory_bed.insight": "Rory’s bed is exactly as Mrs K turned it down at 23:48, when he wasn’t in: the corner folded back, the shortbread on the pillow. Nobody slept in it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_rory_bed.response.0": "…She did my bed, and I never even saw her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_rory_bed.response.1": "All right. I owe a man in London sixty thousand pounds, and he doesn’t send reminders by letter. At twenty to twelve I went out to the car, ran the engine for the heater and sat by the gatepost where the signal is, begging him for another month.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_rory_bed.response.2": "It took me ten minutes to work up the nerve to ring him, and then I was on the phone for twenty. The hotel went dark in the middle of it. I came in at half past twelve and sat in that chair till morning. Here. See for yourself.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_rory_bed.title": "Shortbread on the pillow",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_bar.insight": "Red candle wax on the top of the main stairs. The Great Hall’s candles were white; the red supper candles were in a box behind the bar.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_bar.response.0": "…Yes. I went up.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_bar.response.1": "At five past twelve I thought: Pani Halina is up there alone in the dark. So I took a red candle from the box and went up the main stairs. I called her name along the corridor. Nothing.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_bar.response.2": "At the far end, by the Victoria Suite, I saw a little light. A thin beam, like a pencil. It went into the suite and the door closed. I thought it was her. So I went back down to my bar.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_bar.response.3": "I didn’t tell anyone. A Polish barman upstairs in the dark, the night a diamond goes missing. Who believes him?",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_bar.title": "Red wax on the stairs",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_viv_claim.insight": "In 1985 Dame Vivienne wrote to Peggy: ‘When I’m gone, the Black Ice will be yours.’ Peggy kept the card in her handbag for forty years.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_viv_claim.response.0": "…Oh, Peg.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_viv_claim.response.1": "Archie’s last première. A seam went in the interval, and she sewed me back into the dress in the dark with a needle in her teeth. I was drunk on the applause, and I wrote her that. I’d quite forgotten it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_viv_claim.response.2": "She never said one word about it. Not once in forty years. And last night she clapped.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_viv_claim.title": "A card from 1985",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.conclusion": "The staff cottage has its own meter on the same cable up the glen: when a storm takes the hotel’s power, it takes the cottage’s too. Last night the cottage’s clocks never stopped, while every clock in the hotel was left blinking 00:00. The line never failed. And when Hamish reached the plant room, he found the hotel’s main switch right down at OFF, not halfway, where a trip leaves it, with the plant-room door unlocked. The storm didn’t put the Glenmorran in the dark: somebody went down to the plant room and switched it off.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.options.0": "The storm brought the line down",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.options.1": "The band’s flash blew a fuse",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.options.2": "The old wiring tripped the main switch",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.options.3": "Somebody switched off the hotel’s main switch",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.proofLabels.0": "Whether the line up the glen failed",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.proofLabels.1": "How the switch was found",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.question": "What put the Glenmorran in the dark at 23:58?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dark.title": "Not the storm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.conclusion": "Miss Rennie’s tooth mug is gone from the shelf over her basin. Her window’s catch is up, and a ring of water has dried on the sill. Last night it was eight below at the door, cold enough to shut the pass with black ice. You open the window and brush the snow off the ledge, and there is her tooth mug, frozen solid. Inside the ice, coiled like something asleep, lie a snapped platinum chain and a black stone the size of a quail’s egg. Nobody searching a room thinks to look out of the window, and by morning a mug of water on a Highland ledge had turned the Black Ice into black ice. Miss Rennie is still waiting in the corridor. She told you she never saw Halina. Now you can put the truth to her.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.options.0": "In the lining of her handbag",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.options.1": "Sewn into the hem of her good coat",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.options.2": "Outside her window, on the ledge",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.options.3": "Hidden on the shelves of the linen room",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.proofLabels.0": "What her room tells you",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.proofLabels.1": "How cold the night was",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.question": "Where is the Black Ice now?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_ice.title": "Black ice",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.conclusion": "Mrs K’s last line runs crooked across her list, written by torchlight: ‘Linen room. LIGHTS OUT!’ The lights went out at 23:58. She was alive in the linen room when the hotel went dark, and Dr Shaw’s window of 23:30 to 01:30 fits. She was killed in the dark, by somebody who came to her there.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.options.0": "At 23:15, at the supper, with the Black Ice round her neck",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.options.1": "At 23:45, on the first floor, refusing Mr Munro",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.options.2": "At 23:58, in the linen room, as the lights went out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.options.3": "At 00:40, crossing the yard to the cottage",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.proofLabels.0": "What she wrote last",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.proofLabels.1": "When the lights went out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.question": "When was Mrs K last alive?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_last.title": "Lights out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.conclusion": "The plant room is a minute from the kitchens, three from the Great Hall or the bar, and five from reception. At 23:57:14 everybody still at the supper, guests and staff, was in the band’s photo, too far away to reach the switch by 23:58. Tomasz rang up two drams at the bar at 23:57:52. Hamish was on the phone to the depot at reception until the line died at 23:58. Rory was on his phone at the gatepost from 23:52 to 00:14, and the call drops if you move. That leaves Miss Rennie, who says she was in the empty kitchens, by the cellar stairs, when the lights went out.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.0": "Rory Lisle",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.1": "Peggy Rennie",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.2": "Tomasz Wrona",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.3": "Hamish Grant",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.0": "Who was a minute from the switch",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.1": "Where someone else was at 23:58",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.question": "Who could have been in the plant room at 23:58?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.title": "Two minutes to midnight",
 "dialogues:statements.st_al_bank.text": "At 23:45 I went up and asked Mrs K to lend the hotel the Black Ice as security for the bank. She refused, and I came straight back down.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_al_hall.text": "After the gift I never left the Great Hall.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_ham_off.text": "When I got to the plant room, the main switch was right down at OFF, not halfway as a trip leaves it, and the plant-room door was unlocked.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_ham_storm.text": "The main switch had tripped. Storms trip that switch every winter.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_peggy_confession.text": "I switched off the main switch, struck Halina with her own torch in the linen room, and hid the Black Ice on my window ledge. Vivienne promised it to me.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_peggy_kitchen.text": "At quarter to twelve I went down to the kitchens to heat milk for Vivienne. I was there when the lights went out, and I felt my way back up in the dark. I never saw Halina.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_peggy_suite.text": "I was in the suite from half past eleven until the lights came back.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_peggy_torch.text": "I came up the back stairs by my own torch and went straight to my room. I never went near the linen room, and I never saw Halina.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_rory_bed.text": "I went up at half past eleven and slept through the whole thing.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_rory_car.text": "From twenty to twelve until half past twelve I sat in my car by the gatepost, where the signal is. Most of that time I was on the phone to my bookmaker about my debts.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_bar.text": "In the dark I never left the bar.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_beam.text": "At about eight minutes past twelve I saw a thin torch beam, like a pencil, go into the Victoria Suite at the end of the corridor.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_viv_claim.text": "Rory thinks the Black Ice should have been his. Nobody else had a claim on it.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_viv_promise.text": "In 1985 I wrote Peggy a card promising her the Black Ice once I’m gone. I’d forgotten it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_hotel.lines.0": "My great-grandfather bought it in 1920. Queen Victoria took tea in the library, and Dame Vivienne made her film in the hall. We still turn the beds down with shortbread and a hot water bottle. People come back for fifty years.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_hotel.lines.1": "It’s a hard old place to keep going. The roof alone would break you. But we manage.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_hotel.prompt": "Tell me about the Glenmorran.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_night.lines.0": "Mr Munro, where were you after the gift?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_night.lines.1": "In the Great Hall, running a Burns supper. I never left it. I called the last dance, I was in the band’s photo with Dame Vivienne, and when the lights went I had candles lit inside two minutes.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_night.lines.2": "I counted heads at half past twelve. Mrs K wasn’t there, and I thought nothing of it: she’d often slip across to the cottage after her rounds. God forgive me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_al_night.prompt": "Where were you after the gift?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_halina.lines.0": "Twenty-two years she ran this house. She knew every guest’s pillows and every guest’s secrets, and she never told one of them.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_halina.lines.1": "On a big night she’d do the first floor herself. ‘The best rooms get the best turndown,’ she’d say. Shortbread on the pillow, a hot water bottle in the bed, curtains drawn, and a tick on her list.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_halina.lines.2": "Come March the bank’ll have the place, and there’s no new owner wants a night porter of sixty-three. Mrs K always said she’d see me right. Last night she was wearing the means to do it round her neck.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_halina.prompt": "Tell me about Mrs K.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_night.lines.0": "Mr Grant, what happened when the lights went out?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_night.lines.1": "I was on the phone to the Dalmore depot at the desk. The lights went, and the phone with them. When a storm takes the lights, it’s the line down the glen, and nothing in the plant room will help ye. So I lit the desk candle and went straight out to the stable block for the generator. She wouldn’t start. Twenty minutes in the snow, swearing at her.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_night.lines.2": "Twenty past twelve, I gave up on her and thought: the main switch. Down to the plant room, and sure enough it had tripped. Storms trip that switch every winter. I pushed it up, and we had the lights back at 00:24.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_ham_night.prompt": "What happened when the lights went out?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_night.lines.0": "Miss Rennie, where were you when the lights went out?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_night.lines.1": "Peggy, please. I went up at half past eleven to get Vivienne’s things ready for bed, as I have every night here for forty-five winters. I was in the suite from then until the lights came back.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_night.lines.2": "When they went out I sat very still in the dark and waited. I don’t like the dark. When I’d got my nerve back I came down, and found everybody singing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_night.prompt": "Where were you when the lights went out?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_viv.lines.0": "I’ve dressed her since 1981. Forty quick changes a night in the West End: hooks and eyes in the dark, with my torch in my teeth.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_viv.lines.1": "She touches the pencil torch she wears on a cord round her neck: a habit, she says, from forty years in the wings.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_viv.lines.2": "She’s dying, you know. She doesn’t want a fuss. Giving Halina the Black Ice was so like her: a grand gesture, and no warning to anyone.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_peggy_viv.prompt": "Tell me about Dame Vivienne.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_rory_gift.lines.0": "That stone was my mother’s to inherit. She died when I was nineteen. Gran always said, ‘It’ll come to you, darling.’ And then she hangs it round the housekeeper’s neck in front of a roomful of strangers, with a speech.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_rory_gift.lines.1": "So yes, I stood up and said so, and when the speeches were over I walked out. I’m not proud of it. Gran would have wanted it back by Monday anyway; she always does. But I’d no reason on earth to hurt Mrs K. She brought me hot chocolate when I was eight.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_rory_gift.prompt": "Tell me about the gift.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_rory_night.lines.0": "Mr Lisle, where were you when the lights went out?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_rory_night.lines.1": "In bed. After my little scene at supper I went up at half eleven, poured myself a large one and passed out. I slept through the whole thing. I didn’t know a thing had happened until Peggy banged on my door this morning.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_rory_night.prompt": "Where were you when the lights went out?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_halina.lines.0": "Pani Halina found me this job when I was washing dishes in Inverness. She fed me every Sunday: pierogi, here in the Highlands.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_halina.lines.1": "Last night she showed me the stone under her collar like a schoolgirl. ‘Tomek, what does a housekeeper do with a diamond?’ I said, ‘Sell it, and buy a house.’ She laughed at me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_halina.prompt": "Tell me about Mrs K.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_night.lines.0": "Tomasz, where were you in the dark?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_night.lines.1": "Behind my bar, all the time. When the lights went, I lit candles and kept serving. People want a whisky in the dark. I never left the bar.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_night.prompt": "Where were you in the dark?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_ice.lines.0": "Dame Vivienne, tell me about the Black Ice.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_ice.lines.1": "Archie gave it to me on the day we married, in 1969. He said it looked like black ice on a road: beautiful, and you never see it coming. I wore it in his film, in this very hall.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_ice.lines.2": "In 2004 he came here to die. The snow shut the glen for six weeks and no nurse could get up. Halina sat with him every night. She washed him, she read to him, she held his hand when it happened. I had my diamond, and she had nothing. Now she had it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_ice.lines.3": "Rory thinks it should have been his, and stood up at supper to tell everybody so. Nobody else had a claim on it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_ice.prompt": "Tell me about the Black Ice.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_night.lines.0": "In the middle of the band’s photograph, with Alasdair’s arm round me. The flash went, and a breath later, the dark.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_night.lines.1": "Alasdair lit candles and got everyone round the fire. People were frightened, so I sang them some Burns. I haven’t sung in public since 1979, and I was terrible.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_night.lines.2": "Peggy came down at twenty to one, white as a sheet. She hates the dark, poor lamb; she’d sat it out upstairs on her own. At quarter to one she took me up to bed.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_viv_night.prompt": "Where were you when the lights went out?",
 "evidence:evidence.band_photo.detail": "The Glen Morran Ceilidh Band always ends the night with a photo from the stage. Their leader, Ewan, shows it to you on his camera: Saturday, 23:57:14.\n\nEveryone still at the supper is crammed in front of the stage with their glasses up. Dame Vivienne and Mr Munro are in the middle, arm in arm. Dr Shaw is there, and you are, at the back, with thirteen more guests, and the chef and the waiting staff are by the kitchen door, Kasia among them. Two places at the top table are empty: Rory Lisle walked out at half past eleven, and Miss Rennie went up at the same time, as she does every night, to get Dame Vivienne’s things ready for bed. The bar and reception are out of the shot.\n\n‘I’d hardly put the camera down when the lights went,’ Ewan says. ‘Half the room thought my flash had blown the fuses.’",
 "evidence:evidence.band_photo.name": "The band’s photo",
 "evidence:evidence.band_photo.summary": "The ceilidh band’s end-of-night photo from the stage at 23:57:14. Everyone still at the supper is in it.",
 "evidence:evidence.black_ice.detail": "The card from the hotel’s display case in the Great Hall, where the stone stood for the supper:\n\n‘The Black Ice. A black diamond of 31 carats on a platinum chain, the wedding gift of the director Archie Lisle to Dame Vivienne Lisle, 1969. Worn by Dame Vivienne in The Grey Lady of Glenmorran (1971), filmed in this hall.’\n\nArchie used to say it looked like a puddle of black ice on a road. It is insured for £2.8 million.\n\nAt 23:15 on Saturday Dame Vivienne took it out of the case and fastened it round Mrs K’s neck, in front of the whole supper. Mrs K went red to the ears, tucked it inside her collar, and said she’d take it off when she’d done her rooms.",
 "evidence:evidence.black_ice.name": "The Black Ice",
 "evidence:evidence.black_ice.summary": "A 31-carat black diamond on a platinum chain. At 23:15 Dame Vivienne fastened it round Mrs K’s neck.",
 "evidence:evidence.cottage.detail": "Kasia lets you into the cottage she shared with her mother, across the courtyard from the kitchen door. The cat is asleep on the radiator. Mrs K’s reading glasses are on the arm of her chair.\n\nOn the cooker, the clock says 09:12, right to the minute. So does the radio alarm by her mother’s bed. In the hotel, every clock was blinking 00:00 this morning, until the chef set them again.\n\n‘The cottage has its own meter,’ Kasia says, ‘but it’s the same cable up the glen as the hotel. When a storm takes the hotel’s power, it takes ours too, every time. The last time was in November, and every clock in here was blinking.’\n\n‘I came across at one,’ she says, ‘and Mum wasn’t here. She’d sometimes sit up with Dame Vivienne when the old lady couldn’t sleep, so I went to bed. At six she still wasn’t back, and she’s never once been late for the breakfast trays.’",
 "evidence:evidence.cottage.name": "The staff cottage",
 "evidence:evidence.cottage.summary": "Mrs K’s cottage, across the yard, never lost its power: its clocks are right. It’s on the same cable as the hotel.",
 "evidence:evidence.depot_call.detail": "Mr Munro rang the Dalmore gritting depot at 08:00 to ask about the pass, and wrote down what the duty officer told him:\n\n‘Gritters at the pass by twelve, and your police up by two, if it holds. Funny night, mind. I rang your front desk at five to twelve about this very thing, and your night porter and I were halfway through when the line went dead. Two minutes to midnight, my screen says. I thought, there goes the Glenmorran’s power again.’",
 "evidence:evidence.depot_call.name": "The depot’s call",
 "evidence:evidence.depot_call.summary": "The Dalmore depot’s duty officer was on the phone to Hamish until the line went dead at 23:58.",
 "evidence:evidence.doctors_note.detail": "Notes made at 07:05 on Sunday by Dr Morven Shaw, a GP from Inverness who was at the Burns supper, for the police.\n\nHalina Kowalczyk, 54, head housekeeper. Found by her daughter at 06:40 on the floor of the first-floor linen room, face down by the far shelves.\n\nOne blow to the back of the head with something heavy and rounded. Her own torch, the big rubber kind the hotel keeps for power cuts, lay beside her, switched off. It would fit. She will have died within minutes.\n\nA fresh red line round the back of her neck, where a fine chain was torn away.\n\nTime of death: between 23:30 and 01:30, from her temperature and the stiffness. I can’t be closer than that.\n\nI’ve touched nothing else. Mr Munro has locked the linen room and kept the key.",
 "evidence:evidence.doctors_note.name": "Dr Shaw’s note",
 "evidence:evidence.doctors_note.summary": "Struck once from behind with something heavy and rounded, between 23:30 and 01:30. A chain was torn from her neck.",
 "evidence:evidence.night_book.detail": "The porter’s night book, kept at reception since 1890. Saturday 24 January, in Hamish Grant’s capitals:\n\n21:30 Police have shut the pass. Black ice. No arrivals, no departures. Minus eight at the door.\n23:15 Burns supper: Dame V gave Mrs K the Black Ice, in front of the whole Hall.\n23:55 Dalmore depot on the phone: gritters at the pass by noon tomorrow.\n23:58 LIGHTS OUT. Phone dead.\n00:00 Out to the stable block. Generator won’t start (cold).\n00:24 Plant room: main switch had tripped. Reset. Lights back.\n00:30 Mr Munro’s head count: all present but Mr Lisle (gone to bed), Miss Rennie (upstairs) and Mrs K (across at the cottage?).\n02:00 Snow stopped. Hard frost.\n06:40 Kasia found her mother in the linen room. God rest her.\n07:00 Police told, on the landline. Nobody to leave.",
 "evidence:evidence.night_book.name": "The night book",
 "evidence:evidence.night_book.summary": "Hamish’s log of the night: minus eight at the door, the gift at 23:15, lights out at 23:58, the main switch reset at 00:24.",
 "evidence:evidence.peggy_room.detail": "Miss Rennie’s room, through the connecting door from the Victoria Suite: narrow, warm and neat as a pin. A sewing kit lies open on the table, a nightdress is folded on the pillow, and her good coat hangs on the back of the door. You go through her handbag, the coat’s pockets and every hem in the room. Nothing has been sewn into anything.\n\nOver the basin, her toothbrush lies flat on the glass shelf beside the toothpaste. There’s no tooth mug, there or anywhere else in the room.\n\nThe window looks over the courtyard. Its catch is up, and a ring of water has dried on the sill beneath it. Outside, last night’s snow lies thick and smooth on the ledge.",
 "evidence:evidence.peggy_room.name": "Miss Rennie’s room",
 "evidence:evidence.peggy_room.summary": "Neat as a pin, with nothing sewn into anything. But her tooth mug is missing from the basin, and her window’s catch is up.",
 "evidence:evidence.plant_room.detail": "The hotel’s main switch, on the wall of the plant room: a black box with a lever as long as your hand, ON at the top and OFF at the bottom. Beside it is a card in Hamish’s capitals:\n\n‘IF IT TRIPS THE LEVER SITS HALFWAY. PUSH IT DOWN, THEN UP.’\n\nThe door locks, and the key lives on a hook in the boot room.\n\nFrom the kitchens it’s a minute to the switch: down the cellar stairs and along the passage. From the Great Hall or the bar it’s a good three minutes; from reception, five.",
 "evidence:evidence.plant_room.name": "The main switch",
 "evidence:evidence.plant_room.summary": "A trip leaves the lever halfway. It’s a minute from the kitchens, three from the Great Hall or the bar, and five from reception.",
 "evidence:evidence.promise_card.detail": "In the zipped pocket of Peggy’s handbag, soft at the corners from handling: a card from the première of Archie Lisle’s last film, 1985. In green ink:\n\n‘My darling Peg. Forty quick changes and not one dropped stitch. When I’m gone, the Black Ice will be yours. I mean it. V.’",
 "evidence:evidence.promise_card.name": "The 1985 card",
 "evidence:evidence.promise_card.summary": "A card from Dame Vivienne to Peggy, 1985: ‘When I’m gone, the Black Ice will be yours.’",
 "evidence:evidence.rory_bed.detail": "The Stag Room, at the east end of the corridor. The bed is exactly as Mrs K left it at turndown: the covers folded back in a neat triangle, a hot water bottle, long cold, under the sheet, and a round of shortbread in its waxed paper on the pillow. Nobody has slept in it, or even sat on it.\n\nOver the armchair by the window hang a dinner jacket and a bow tie. On the sill is a whisky glass, dried to a brown ring.",
 "evidence:evidence.rory_bed.name": "Rory’s bed",
 "evidence:evidence.rory_bed.summary": "The Stag Room’s bed is still turned down: the corner folded back, and Mrs K’s shortbread on the pillow.",
 "evidence:evidence.rory_calls.detail": "Rory shows you the call log on his phone for Saturday night:\n\n23:52 · Tony B (mobile) · 22 minutes\n\n‘There’s one bar of signal in this whole glen, by the gatepost in the car park. You sit in the car with the engine on for the heater, and you don’t move, or you lose it. Ring Tony if you like. He’ll tell you I never stopped talking.’",
 "evidence:evidence.rory_calls.name": "Rory’s phone",
 "evidence:evidence.rory_calls.summary": "Rory’s calls on Saturday night: twenty-two minutes to his bookmaker, from 23:52 to 00:14.",
 "evidence:evidence.till_roll.detail": "The end of Saturday’s till roll, which Tomasz tears off for you:\n\n23:51:30 · 1 × Talisker 10 · £9.00\n23:54:12 · 2 × Glenmorran 12 · £19.00\n23:57:52 · 2 × Laphroaig 10 · £18.00 · cash · served by TW\n\nNothing after that. The till died with the lights at 23:58. In the dark Tomasz kept serving by candlelight, and took the money in a pint glass.",
 "evidence:evidence.till_roll.name": "The bar’s till roll",
 "evidence:evidence.till_roll.summary": "The last sale before the lights went out: two drams, rung up by Tomasz at 23:57:52.",
 "evidence:evidence.turndown_list.detail": "The clipboard on Mrs K’s trolley, in the first-floor corridor. Saturday’s turndown list, in her quick, sloping hand:\n\n23:31 Heather (Dr Shaw) ✓\n23:36 Thistle ✓\n23:41 Rowan ✓\n23:45 Mr M came up. The bank. NO.\n23:48 Stag (Mr Lisle) ✓ Not in. Shortbread on pillow.\n23:52 Victoria Suite & Miss Rennie ✓ Nobody in. Bottles in both beds.\n23:58 Linen room. LIGHTS OUT!\n\nThe last line runs crooked across the page, as if it was written by torchlight.",
 "evidence:evidence.turndown_list.name": "Mrs K’s turndown list",
 "evidence:evidence.turndown_list.summary": "Her clipboard from Saturday night: each first-floor room with the time she did it, and a last line at 23:58.",
 "evidence:evidence.wax.detail": "Three drops of red candle wax on the top steps of the main stairs, and a fourth on the first-floor landing, hard in the tartan carpet.\n\nWhen the lights went out, the candles handed round in the Great Hall were the hotel’s plain white ones, in silver sticks. The only red candles in the building were the supper’s table candles, in tartan holders. After the supper they went back in their box behind the bar.",
 "evidence:evidence.wax.name": "Red wax on the stairs",
 "evidence:evidence.wax.summary": "Red candle wax on the top steps of the main stairs. The candles in the Great Hall were white; the red ones were at the bar.",
 "suspects:suspects.alasdair.bio": "The third Munro to own the Glenmorran, and the man who made its Burns supper famous. He was master of ceremonies on Saturday night. When the lights went out, he had candles lit in two minutes and everyone gathered round the Great Hall fire.",
 "suspects:suspects.alasdair.dismissal": "I’m afraid I don’t follow you.",
 "suspects:suspects.alasdair.name": "Alasdair Munro",
 "suspects:suspects.alasdair.rebuttal": "This hotel is my family’s life, and Halina was part of it for twenty-two years. I spent that whole night in the Great Hall with a candle and thirty frightened people.",
 "suspects:suspects.alasdair.role": "The owner",
 "suspects:suspects.hamish.bio": "Night porter at the Glenmorran for thirty-one years. He keeps the night book at reception, and knows where every key in the building hangs. When the lights went out he fought the old generator in the snow, and at 00:24 he got the lights back on.",
 "suspects:suspects.hamish.dismissal": "Ye’ve lost me there.",
 "suspects:suspects.hamish.name": "Hamish Grant",
 "suspects:suspects.hamish.rebuttal": "Thirty-one years I’ve kept this door. I was out in the snow swearing at a generator while somebody did that to her.",
 "suspects:suspects.hamish.role": "The night porter",
 "suspects:suspects.peggy.bio": "Dame Vivienne’s dresser since 1981, and her companion now: forty-five winters at the Glenmorran. She sleeps in the small room that connects to the Victoria Suite, and has never once been late with a hem.",
 "suspects:suspects.peggy.dismissal": "I’m sorry, dear. I don’t follow.",
 "suspects:suspects.peggy.name": "Peggy Rennie",
 "suspects:suspects.peggy.rebuttal": "Halina was my friend for twenty years. I’m sixty-eight, and I dress an old lady for a living. Look at me.",
 "suspects:suspects.peggy.role": "The dresser",
 "suspects:suspects.rory.bio": "Dame Vivienne’s only grandson, a London property agent between jobs. The Black Ice was his late mother’s to inherit, and then, he always understood, his. At the supper he stood up, said so to the whole room, and walked out.",
 "suspects:suspects.rory.dismissal": "Not a clue what you’re on about.",
 "suspects:suspects.rory.name": "Rory Lisle",
 "suspects:suspects.rory.rebuttal": "I wanted that stone, and everybody knows it. That’s not the same as killing a woman for it.",
 "suspects:suspects.rory.role": "The grandson",
 "suspects:suspects.tomasz.bio": "From Kraków, like Mrs K, who found him the job four years ago and fed him every Sunday since. He runs the Glenmorran’s bar of four hundred whiskies, and on Saturday night he kept it open by candlelight.",
 "suspects:suspects.tomasz.dismissal": "Sorry, I don’t understand.",
 "suspects:suspects.tomasz.name": "Tomasz Wrona",
 "suspects:suspects.tomasz.rebuttal": "She was like my mother here. I would never touch her. Never.",
 "suspects:suspects.tomasz.role": "The barman",
 "suspects:suspects.vivienne.bio": "The star of The Grey Lady of Glenmorran, filmed in the hotel’s Great Hall in 1971, and its most famous guest for more than fifty winters. Her husband, the director Archie Lisle, died here in 2004. She is very ill, and says so. At the Burns supper she gave away the Black Ice.",
 "suspects:suspects.vivienne.dismissal": "Darling, I haven’t the faintest idea what you mean.",
 "suspects:suspects.vivienne.name": "Dame Vivienne Lisle",
 "suspects:suspects.vivienne.rebuttal": "I sat in that hall in the dark and sang to frightened people. I gave Halina the stone because she deserved it. I’d have given her anything.",
 "suspects:suspects.vivienne.role": "The film star"
}
